   <dc:title>
    <rdf:Alt>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">A Brace-Integrated Fiber Bragg Grating Sensor with a Multi-Hardness Silicone Strain Transfer Substrate for Knee Flexion Monitoring: A Proof-of-Concept Study</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">A Brace-Integrated Fiber Bragg Grating Sensor with a Multi-Hardness Silicone Strain Transfer Substrate for Knee Flexion Monitoring: A Proof-of-Concept Study</rdf:li>
    </rdf:Alt>
   </dc:title>
   <dc:subject>
    <rdf:Bag>
     <rdf:li>fiber Bragg grating sensor; knee flexion monitoring; wearable sensor; knee brace; silicone strain transfer substrate; gait analysis</rdf:li>
    </rdf:Bag>
   </dc:subject>
   <dc:type>
    <rdf:Bag>
     <rdf:li>Text</rdf:li>
    </rdf:Bag>
   </dc:type>
   <dc:description>
    <rdf:Alt>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">Continuous knee flexion monitoring is important for rehabilitation and gait analysis</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">Continuous knee flexion monitoring is important for rehabilitation and gait analysis</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">but optical motion capture is generally confined to laboratories</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">but optical motion capture is generally confined to laboratories</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">and wearable electrical sensors may be affected by drift</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">and wearable electrical sensors may be affected by drift</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">misalignment</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">misalignment</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">or electromagnetic interference. This study presents a brace-integrated system based on a fiber Bragg grating (FBG) embedded in a functionally segmented</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">or electromagnetic interference. This study presents a brace-integrated system based on a fiber Bragg grating (FBG) embedded in a functionally segmented</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">multi-hardness silicone strain transfer substrate. The substrate was placed in a dedicated brace recess</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">multi-hardness silicone strain transfer substrate. The substrate was placed in a dedicated brace recess</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">avoiding a separate rigid transduction mechanism. A portable interrogation unit enabled wireless transmission and real-time brace-derived angle estimation during sit-to-stand and walking tasks. Calibration against brace hinge angles from 0Â° to 90Â° showed a nonlinear Bragg wavelength--angle relationship</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">avoiding a separate rigid transduction mechanism. A portable interrogation unit enabled wireless transmission and real-time brace-derived angle estimation during sit-to-stand and walking tasks. Calibration against brace hinge angles from 0Â° to 90Â° showed a nonlinear Bragg wavelength--angle relationship</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">modeled using a fourth-order polynomial whose order was selected using a separately acquired validation set that was also used for performance assessment. Validation based on 10 angle-wise mean estimates yielded R2 = 0.9992</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">modeled using a fourth-order polynomial whose order was selected using a separately acquired validation set that was also used for performance assessment. Validation based on 10 angle-wise mean estimates yielded R2 = 0.9992</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">RMSE = 0.90Â°</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">RMSE = 0.90Â°</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">and MAE = 0.68Â°</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">and MAE = 0.68Â°</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">with Bland--Altman 95% limits of agreement of $-$1.46Â° to +2.05Â°. The 0Â° stability record had a wavelength standard deviation of 1.41 pm and a linear trend of $-$0.193 pm/min</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">with Bland--Altman 95% limits of agreement of $-$1.46Â° to +2.05Â°. The 0Â° stability record had a wavelength standard deviation of 1.41 pm and a linear trend of $-$0.193 pm/min</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">and Allan deviation analysis gave a wavelength noise floor of 0.089 pm at an averaging time of 24 s. Noise-equivalent angular variation ranged from 0.08Â° to 0.52Â°. During a sustained 30Â° hold</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">and Allan deviation analysis gave a wavelength noise floor of 0.089 pm at an averaging time of 24 s. Noise-equivalent angular variation ranged from 0.08Â° to 0.52Â°. During a sustained 30Â° hold</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">the estimated angle drifted at $-$0.089Â°/min</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">the estimated angle drifted at $-$0.089Â°/min</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">but comparison of the 0Â° and 30Â° records in wavelength units showed that the undeformed condition reproduced approximately 59% of that drift</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">but comparison of the 0Â° and 30Â° records in wavelength units showed that the undeformed condition reproduced approximately 59% of that drift</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">although the non-simultaneous comparison does not establish a common-mode thermal contribution. An uncompensated temperature change of 1 K corresponds to between 0.31Â° and 1.98Â° of apparent angle change. Proof-of-concept measurements in two participants</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">although the non-simultaneous comparison does not establish a common-mode thermal contribution. An uncompensated temperature change of 1 K corresponds to between 0.31Â° and 1.98Â° of apparent angle change. Proof-of-concept measurements in two participants</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">both of whom are authors</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">both of whom are authors</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">produced brace-derived profiles qualitatively consistent with expected task kinematics but revealed a cumulative zero-offset shift of 3.2Â° across two sit-to-stand cycles. Because no simultaneous anatomical reference was acquired</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">produced brace-derived profiles qualitatively consistent with expected task kinematics but revealed a cumulative zero-offset shift of 3.2Â° across two sit-to-stand cycles. Because no simultaneous anatomical reference was acquired</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">the dynamic results establish feasibility and waveform plausibility rather than measurement accuracy. The results support the engineering feasibility of this brace-integrated optical architecture</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">the dynamic results establish feasibility and waveform plausibility rather than measurement accuracy. The results support the engineering feasibility of this brace-integrated optical architecture</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">while identifying temperature compensation and cyclic loading characterization as prerequisites for anatomical and clinical validation.</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">while identifying temperature compensation and cyclic loading characterization as prerequisites for anatomical and clinical validation.</rdf:li>
    </rdf:Alt>
   </dc:description>
